*Applies **jvm-internals-and-garbage-collection** to the decision above.*

**Observable outcome:** You can explain how “Deep Dive: Detecting SRP Violations” changes the architecture or implementation path.

## Stage 4 — SRP Responsibility Flow

- When a request hits UserRegistrationService.register() , responsibility flows cleanly to dedicated single-purpose classes — each is an isolated change axis.
- If the database schema changes, only UserRepository is modified.
- If the email template changes, only WelcomeEmailService is touched.
- The orchestrator UserRegistrationService never changes for infrastructure reasons — only if the registration workflow itself changes.

*Applies **low-level-design-systems** to the decision above.*

```mermaid
flowchart LR
    A["register(user)"] --> B[UserRepository.save]
    A --> C[WelcomeEmailService.sendWelcome]
    A --> D[UserAuditLogger.logCreated]
    B --> E[(Database)]
    C --> F[Email Provider]
    D --> G[Audit Log]
```

**Observable outcome:** The orchestrator UserRegistrationService never changes for infrastructure reasons — only if the registration workflow itself changes.

## Stage 5 — Real-World Application: SRP at Scale

- SRP applies at every scale — class, service, and infrastructure.
- Where to apply SRP first: Start at the class level.
- Pain signals: unit tests with 5+ mocks in setUp, class names with "And" or "Manager", files frequently edited by two different teams for unrelated features.
- At the microservice level, SRP manifests as bounded contexts — each service owns one business capability.

*Applies **design-principles-solid** to the decision above.*

**Observable outcome:** At the microservice level, SRP manifests as bounded contexts — each service owns one business capability.

## Stage 6 — Hands-On: Identify and Refactor SRP Violations

- Here is a class that violates SRP.
- Identify the responsibilities, then split them.
- Five responsibilities → five focused classes: Orchestrator: OrderProcessingService has one responsibility: orchestrating the order workflow.
- Each collaborator has one responsibility: doing its specific job.

*Applies **event-driven-architecture** to the decision above.*

```java
// 🛑 How many responsibilities does this class have?
class OrderProcessor {
    public void processOrder(Order order) {
        // Responsibility 1: Validate
        if (order.getItems().isEmpty()) {
            throw new IllegalArgumentException("Order has no items");
        }
        // Responsibility 2: Charge
        stripeClient.charge(order.getCustomerId(), order.getTotal());
        // Responsibility 3: Update inventory
        for (Item item : order.getItems()) {
            inventoryDb.decrementStock(item.getSku(), item.getQuantity());
        }
        // Responsibility 4: Notify
        emailService.send(order.getEmail(), "Order confirmed", buildBody(order));
        // Responsibility 5: Audit
        logger.info("Order processed: " + order.getId());
    }
}
```

```java
class OrderProcessingService {
    public void processOrder(Order order) {
        validator.validate(order);
        paymentService.charge(order);
        inventoryService.decrementStock(order);
        notificationService.sendConfirmation(order);
        auditLogger.logProcessed(order);
    }
}
```

**Observable outcome:** Each collaborator has one responsibility: doing its specific job.

## End-to-end outcome

After working through the stages, you should be able to explain:

- - The "And" test always works.
- If you cannot name a class without "And" or "Manager", it has multiple responsibilities.

